  "query_preview": "..A shortwave ejecting off a parent trough currently\noff the PacNW coast will continue to move eastward across the\nregion today. Modest instability from this shortwave will allow\nisolated showers and thunderstorms to develop this afternoon along\nand east of the Wasatch into the Uinta Basin, as well as portions\nof southeast Utah. The Storm Prediction Center has most of eastern\nUtah in a Marginal (level 1 of 5) Risk for these storms to produce\ngusty outflow winds in excess of 58 mph. Conditions across the\nremainder of Utah and southwest Wyoming stay dry today, with\nwidespread gusty southwesterly winds 30-45 mph prevailing as a \nresult of the aforementioned trough. \n\nThe parent trough gradually digs southward along the coast of \nCalifornia Thursday through Saturday, keeping winds elevated\nacross the region each afternoon and evening through this period,\nespecially Thursday and Friday across the western half of Utah.\nWhile winds will be gusty each day, gusts in excess of 45 mph are\nnot likely to be widespread and persistent enough, thus not\nwarranting any headlines at this time. A drier airmass and more \nlimited instability will limit chances for afternoon convection\nacross the region through this period.\n\nThe weather over this upcoming holiday weekend will be overall\ndictated by the progression of what is now Tropical Storm Marie,\ncurrently situated south of the Baja Peninsula. Prior guidance\nindicated the advection of deep moisture associated with this \nstorm as early as Saturday, though recent guidance has backed off\non this scenario, and delays deepest moisture transport until\nSunday. With the aforementioned trough expected to begin gradually\nshifting eastward Sunday, ample instability will exist across the\nregion for widespread showers and thunderstorms to develop,\nbringing an increased risk of heavy rainfall and flash flooding to\nthe region. Even small changes to the track of the tropical system\ncould greatly change the magnitude of the moisture influx into our\nregion late this weekend into early next week, so stay tuned to\nthe forecast as we get closer.",
